{"id":"n-20261011-cgta1p","category":"health","display_title":"Global Threats Facing Humanity by 2100","display_title_he":"האיומים הגלובליים המרחפים על האנושות עד 2100","short_summary":"A landmark Lancet commission warns that nuclear war, malicious AI, and falling aid spending pose catastrophic risks to humanity over the next 75 years.","english_level":"C1","words":508,"reading_time":4,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/CSAF_%26_CMSAF_visits_the_Global_Gateway_%288805195%29.jpg/960px-CSAF_%26_CMSAF_visits_the_Global_Gateway_%288805195%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"U.S. Air Force photo by Airman 1st Class Trevor Calvert · Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CSAF_%26_CMSAF_visits_the_Global_Gateway_(8805195).jpg"},"sources":[{"name":"The Guardian","domain":"theguardian.com","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2026/oct/11/nuclear-war-and-malicious-use-of-ai-threaten-end-of-humanity-by-2100-says-lancet-assessment","title":"Nuclear war and malicious use of AI threaten end of humanity by 2100, says Lancet assessment","published_at":"2026-10-10T23:01:09.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-10T23:01:09.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-11T05:59:34.616Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":9,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["Humanity faces a complex web of interrelated perils that could shape or even terminate its survival before the close of the century, according to an extensive assessment published by The Lancet Commission on 21st Century Global Threats to Health. Comprising thirty-five international experts, the panel identified seventeen major catastrophic threats over the next seventy-five years. Chief among these existential hazards are the malicious deployment of artificial intelligence and nuclear warfare. Although researchers consider their probability low, both carry genuine risks of human extinction by the year 2100. A nuclear exchange could inject massive quantities of debris into the upper atmosphere, blocking sunlight and triggering catastrophic famine, while unchecked artificial intelligence could facilitate the creation of lethal biological weapons. An accompanying Lancet editorial cautioned that the resulting devastation would inflict an apocalypse on humanity.","To evaluate these diverse dangers, the commission modeled disability-adjusted life years, a standardized metric measuring healthy years forfeited to premature death, disability, or prolonged illness. Beyond existential extinction events, the panel identified fifteen additional threats projected to individually cause more than one billion cumulative lost healthy years. This expansive inventory includes climate breakdown, conventional warfare, air pollution, antimicrobial resistance, and naturally occurring pandemics, alongside widespread physiological and societal challenges such as high blood pressure, excessive alcohol consumption, smoking, obesity, poor mental health, income inequality, and unhealthy ageing.","The findings, scheduled for presentation at the World Health Summit in Berlin, emphasize that these crises interact dynamically and cannot be tackled in isolation. Furthermore, their burdens diverge sharply across economic divides. While affluent populations struggle primarily with non-communicable conditions like obesity, lower-income regions endure severe health deficits fueled by low educational attainment. Compounding these disparities, major donor nations—notably the United States and the United Kingdom—have announced steep reductions in overseas assistance over the past year. Analysts warned that the erosion of international health aid could cause up to 1.5 billion lost healthy years before 2100, with nearly seventy percent concentrated in sub-Saharan Africa, which already bears the brunt of childhood malnutrition.","Despite these grim projections, the commissioners stressed that disaster is not foreordained, noting that a realistic route to act exists for every peril evaluated. In fact, viable solutions were a strict prerequisite for inclusion on the list. For example, aggressive intervention to cap global heating at approximately 1.8 degrees Celsius rather than permitting it to reach 4.4 degrees could avert 1.89 billion lost years of healthy life. Professor Christopher Murray, commission co-chair and director of the Institute for Health Metrics and Evaluation at the University of Washington, affirmed that decisions made by contemporary leaders will profoundly shape the health and wellbeing of future generations. Professor Ibrahim Abubakar of University College London reiterated that the objective is to empower policymakers with measurable leverage rather than induce paralyzing panic.","Initially launched three years ago to examine conflict, climate, and pandemics, the commission's scope broadened after realizing the true magnitude of global hazards. With annual updates planned to incorporate emerging data, the authors urged governments to invest in technological innovation, fortify health systems, and allocate personnel and funds toward proactive prevention and preparation."],"facts":[{"fact":"A new assessment by The Lancet Commission on 21st Century Global Threats to Health identifies 17 catastrophic risks facing humanity over the next 75 years.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The report warns that the malicious use of AI, such as AI-created bioweapons, and nuclear conflict resulting in nuclear famine could cause human extinction by 2100, though their likelihood remains low.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The commission of 35 experts modeled disability-adjusted life years (DALYs) to evaluate the burden of each threat, finding that several hazards could each cause over 1 billion lost years of healthy life.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The 17 major threats include climate breakdown, declining foreign aid, air pollution, antimicrobial resistance, childhood malnutrition, conventional conflict, high blood pressure, and mental health issues.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Limiting global heating to roughly 1.8C rather than 4.4C could avert 1.89 billion lost years of healthy life.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Steep cuts to international health aid by major donors like the US and UK could cause up to 1.5 billion DALYs by 2100, with nearly 70% concentrated in sub-Saharan Africa.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The commission emphasizes that realistic solutions exist for every identified threat and plans to update its findings annually following its presentation at the World Health Summit in Berlin.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"humanity","lemma":"humanity","pos":"noun","he":"האנושות","name":false},{"text":"faces","lemma":"face","pos":"verb","he":"ניצבת בפני, מתמודדת עם","name":false},{"text":"complex","lemma":"complex","pos":"adj","he":"מורכבת, מסובכת","name":false},{"text":"web","lemma":"web","pos":"noun","he":"רשת","name":false},{"text":"interrelated","lemma":"interrelated","pos":"adj","he":"קשורים זה בזה, שלובים","name":false},{"text":"perils","lemma":"peril","pos":"noun","he":"סכנות, איומים","name":false},{"text":"shape","lemma":"shape","pos":"verb","he":"לעצב","name":false},{"text":"terminate","lemma":"terminate","pos":"verb","he":"לשים קץ ל-, לקטוע","name":false},{"text":"survival","lemma":"survival","pos":"noun","he":"הישרדות","name":false},{"text":"close of the century","lemma":"close of the century","pos":"phrase","he":"סוף המאה","name":false},{"text":"extensive","lemma":"extensive","pos":"adj","he":"מקיף, נרחב","name":false},{"text":"assessment","lemma":"assessment","pos":"noun","he":"הערכה, אומדן","name":false},{"text":"published","lemma":"publish","pos":"verb","he":"פורסם","name":false},{"text":"The Lancet Commission on 21st Century Global Threats to Health","lemma":"The Lancet Commission on 21st Century Global Threats to Health","pos":"noun","he":"ועדת לנסט לאיומי בריאות עולמיים במאה ה-21","nam